course: dictionary
lesson: 11259
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Wrack is one of them. To find the right meaning, read the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues.
explain: As a noun, wrack means seaweed, especially the brown kind that grows on rocks or washes up on the beach. «Seaweeds are algae with no roots, only a holdfast gripping the rock, and bladder wrack's air bubbles lift it towards the light when the tide is in.» The sentence is about the sea and the tide, so wrack means seaweed.
explain: Now look at «wrack and ruin». Wrack has nothing to do with seaweed here. It means falling to pieces because nobody cares for something. Ask yourself: does my meaning fit the sentence? Swap it in and check.
fact: Wrack is a noun that means seaweed, especially brown seaweed on rocks or washed up on the beach.
fact: Bladder wrack is a seaweed with air bubbles that help it float in the water.
fact: «Wrack and ruin» means a state of falling to pieces from neglect, as an old house can in a story.
fact: «Wracked with pain» means tormented by pain. Here wrack is another spelling of rack.
fact: «Nerve-wracking» describes something that causes strong worry.
exercise: «bladder wrack's air bubbles lift it towards the light when the tide is in.» In this sentence, what is wrack? | A tall rock on the shore | A small crab | *Seaweed, such as bladder wrack | Sand that the tide has washed away
exercise: An old house «goes to wrack and ruin» over many years. What does wrack and ruin mean? | *Falling to pieces from neglect | Sold quickly to a new family | Filled with seaweed from the sea | Painted bright and new
exercise: «She was wracked with pain after the fall.» What does wracked mean here? | Shaped like a shell | Washed up on the beach | Floating in the water | *Tormented by
test: «The beach was covered in wrack after the storm.» What is wrack in this sentence? | Stones | Rubbish left by people | *Seaweed | Sand
test: «The final match was nerve-wracking.» What does nerve-wracking suggest? | Very relaxing | Full of seaweed | *Causing strong worry | Funny
exam: Mia reads «The old mill had gone to wrack and ruin.» Which meaning fits best? | *Falling to pieces from neglect | Shining and new | Tormented by pain | Covered in seaweed
